-- Passage rg10-word-parts-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('3a9ed744-5fee-5a76-b9b6-426a3d196390', 'rg10-word-parts-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-word-parts'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Reading the River$q$, $q$Along the banks of the Calloway River, a small metal shelter holds one of the most useful instruments in the county: a stream gauge that records the water level every fifteen minutes. The hydrologist who maintains it, Dr. Amara Okafor, describes the gauge as the river's pulse. When the numbers climb quickly, she knows that rain falling on the hills upstream is already on its way down.

The terrain around the river shapes how fast that water arrives. Steep, rocky slopes shed rain almost immediately, while flat meadows and wetlands hold it back like a sponge. After a long drought, however, even meadow soil can turn hard and dehydrated, and the first heavy storm runs straight off the surface instead of soaking in.

Okafor also measures what the river carries. On a calm day the water looks nearly clear, but after a storm it turns the color of coffee with milk. That cloudiness comes from suspended sediment, tiny particles of clay and silt that hang in the moving water instead of settling to the bottom. By filtering and weighing samples, her team can estimate how much soil the watershed loses each year.

Not all of the river's water is visible. A large share travels through subterranean layers of gravel and sand, feeding the stream slowly even during dry weeks. Wells drilled near the banks let the team track this hidden flow.

The data serve a practical purpose. When readings suggest an impending flood, the county sends alerts to farms and neighborhoods downstream, often hours before the water reaches them. Okafor points out that a forecast is only as good as the record behind it, which is why the gauge has run without a break for more than thirty years.$q$, 'draft')
