-- Passage rg10-word-parts-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('edfad48f-1897-5b29-a6df-497e5fa046bb', 'rg10-word-parts-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-word-parts'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Librarian's Letters$q$, $q$Every Thursday after school, Priya Raman climbed the narrow stairs of the Harlow County Historical Society to a room that smelled of old paper. Her job was to transcribe a box of letters written in the 1880s by a woman named Josephine Abara, who had run the town's first lending library.

The director, Mr. Szabo, had given Priya one firm rule on her first day. "Copy exactly what you see," he said. "Fidelity to the original matters more than neat spelling. If Josephine wrote a word wrong, you write it wrong too, and add a note." At first Priya found this frustrating, but she soon understood that every misspelling was a small clue about how Josephine had learned to write.

The letters revealed that the library had begun in a temporary space, a corner of the general store, until a local benefactor donated a brick building on Main Street. Josephine never named this donor publicly. In one letter, marked confidential across the top, she explained that the gift came from a mill owner who wanted no credit for it.

Priya was surprised by how lively the letters were. Josephine argued with her contemporaries about which books a small town needed, insisting that farmers' children deserved atlases and chemistry texts as much as anyone. Some of her neighbors thought this was a waste of money. She answered them with patience, and with numbers showing how many children borrowed the atlases each week.

By November, Priya had finished forty letters. Mr. Szabo read her transcriptions and nodded slowly. "You have kept her voice," he said. Priya realized that was exactly the point: more than a century later, Josephine still sounded like herself.$q$, 'draft')
