-- Passage rg10-word-parts-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('561426fe-8037-5ada-8121-ba0a8b7ad0a4', 'rg10-word-parts-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-word-parts'), null,
        '9-12', $q$A Bridge Over the Rail Yard$q$, $q$For decades, the only way to cross the Millbrook rail yard on foot was a long detour around its eastern end, nearly a mile out of the way. Students walking to Westfield High often ignored the detour and cut between parked freight cars, a habit that worried both parents and railroad workers.

Eventually the city council approved a pedestrian bridge that would carry walkers and cyclists over the tracks. Planners knew the project would face obstacles. The rail yard could not simply shut down, so construction had to fit around train schedules, and any delay that might impede freight traffic would cost the railroad money.

The engineers solved this problem with careful planning. Rather than building the bridge in place, crews assembled the main span in a nearby parking lot. Then, during a single overnight closure, two enormous cranes lifted the span and set it onto concrete towers. The work had to follow a strict sequence: towers first, then the span, then railings and lights.

To expedite the final stage, the city hired a second crew to install lighting while the first crew finished the ramps. The bridge opened three weeks ahead of schedule.

The consequences were noticeable almost immediately. Counters mounted on the railings recorded more than two thousand crossings in the first week. In the subsequent school year, the district reported that students living west of the yard arrived on time more often, and no one had been seen walking between the freight cars.

Not every result was planned. Local runners adopted the bridge as part of a popular loop, and a small coffee cart now parks at the western ramp each morning, serving walkers on their way to work.$q$, 'draft')
