-- Passage rg10-word-parts-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('d0803843-f475-5e37-85c5-18ed26350bae', 'rg10-word-parts-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-word-parts'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Writing Without a Name$q$, $q$Not every book cover tells the truth about who wrote the book. For centuries, authors have published under invented names, and the reasons are as varied as the writers themselves.

A pseudonym, or pen name, can protect a writer's privacy. A schoolteacher who wrote adventure novels at night might have worried that parents would not take her seriously in the classroom. Other writers adopted pen names because publishers believed readers would expect a certain kind of author for a certain kind of story. Some simply wanted a fresh start after an earlier book had been poorly reviewed.

Going further, some writers chose to remain anonymous, publishing with no name at all. Many early newspaper essays, pamphlets, and old folk ballads have no known author. Scholars who study these texts sometimes try to identify the writer by comparing word choices, spelling habits, and favorite expressions with those of known authors. A writer who always used one unusual word, never its common synonym, can leave a trail as clear as a fingerprint.

Names can also be built from other names. A writing group might call itself by an acronym formed from the first letters of its members' last names. A character so memorable that a whole series is named for her becomes the eponymous heroine of the books.

Today, online writers often post under usernames, which work much like pen names. The questions remain familiar ones. How much should readers know about the person behind the words? Does a story change when we learn who wrote it? Readers have argued about those questions for generations, and they will likely keep arguing.$q$, 'draft')
