  ('d6dcdc71-7eda-522d-bcf1-c92e26018058', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-word-parts'), null, 'medium',
   $q$The prefix pre- means "before," and the root jud means "judge." What is Wen warning against when he says "Don't prejudge the answer"?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Asking too many questions of the business office","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"context-only-guess"},{"text":"Judging the article after it is already printed","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-prefix"},{"text":"Waiting too long before turning in the article","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"confuses-related-idea"},{"text":"Deciding what is true before getting the facts","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Deciding what is true before getting the facts$q$, $q$To prejudge is to judge "before" knowing the facts, and Wen adds "don't assume anyone lied." Judging after printing reverses the meaning of pre-, which means before.$q$, 'draft', '5a67b95b-ebcb-5bb8-9b9d-986e52e1e701')
