-- Passage rg10-word-parts-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('cf302700-e714-5a6b-801b-1fb4a7876d9d', 'rg10-word-parts-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-word-parts'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Opening Night, Backstage$q$, $q$Three hours before the curtain rose on the spring play, Kofi Asante stood on a ladder in the wings, checking the cables that held the painted moon. The moon hung twenty feet above the stage, and in the second act it had to descend slowly while the lead actor delivered a long monologue about leaving home.

Kofi was the stage manager, which meant that nearly everything rested on him. He kept a thick binder called the prompt book, filled with every cue for lights, sound, and scenery. Ms. Lindqvist, the drama teacher, liked to say that a dependable stage manager was worth more than a famous actor.

The play opened with a short prologue in which a narrator introduced the town and its people, and it closed with an epilogue set ten years later. Between them were twenty scene changes, and the crew had practiced each one until they could do it in the dark.

This year's opening night was also a benefit performance. All of the ticket money would go to the town's animal shelter, and volunteers had set up a table in the lobby with photographs of dogs and cats waiting for homes. The cast had agreed that a full house would be the most beneficial thing they could do for the shelter.

At seven o'clock the house lights dimmed. Kofi pressed his headset button and whispered, "Stand by, lights one." The moon came down right on time during the monologue, glowing softly. When the narrator stepped forward for the epilogue, Kofi finally let himself relax.

Afterward, the shelter director told the cast that the evening had raised enough to care for every animal through the summer.$q$, 'draft')
